    "stability": "experimental",
    "notes": "Recorded from `codex app-server` 0.150.1 driven live over stdio JSON-RPC against a disposable git repository under TMPDIR, never the AutoStack checkout. Machine-specific values are normalized to TRANSCRIPT_PLACEHOLDERS in both the frames and the recorded argv: the temp repository to /tmp/agent-workspace, the home directory to /home/agent, the username to agent. `-c mcp_servers={}` is part of every recorded argv because the operator's own MCP servers otherwise spam `mcpServer/startupStatus/updated` notifications and stderr auth failures into the transcript; it is the Codex analogue of Claude's `--setting-sources project`. WIRE SHAPE, VERBATIM: Codex app-server responses and notifications carry NO `jsonrpc` member -- responses are {\"id\":n,\"result\":{...}} and notifications are {\"method\":...,\"params\":...,\"emittedAtMs\":n}. Only the CLIENT's outbound messages carry \"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\" in the recorded driver. A parser that requires `jsonrpc` on inbound frames will reject every real Codex frame. `initialize` returns {userAgent, codexHome, platformFamily, platformOs} and negotiates NOTHING: unlike ACP, Codex advertises no capabilities, so the Codex descriptor must be derived from the launch profile (app-server versus exec) rather than negotiated. `turn/completed` is a TURN boundary, not a session boundary -- `completed` is minted on session end, exactly as for Claude's `result`. Response ids are as recorded (initialize 0, thread/start 1, turn/start 2), so a replaying client must mint request ids in the same order; the awaitStdin matches are structural subsets on `method`, so they do not pin ids the client mints. PERMISSION CHANNEL: with `approvalPolicy: \"on-request\"` and `sandbox: \"read-only\"`, a write attempt produces a server->client REQUEST -- it carries an `id`, so it MUST be answered, and the turn does not proceed until it is. `availableDecisions` maps onto AgentPermissionOption as accept -> allow_once, acceptWithExecpolicyAmendment -> allow_always, cancel -> deny_once, which satisfies the contract's must-offer-a-denial refinement. A `serverRequest/resolved` notification follows the reply and is the natural source for `permission_resolved`. D-3 ORDERING: the approval request precedes every frame that would map to a `tool_call`; the `item/started` and `item/completed` commandExecution frames and the fileChange frame all arrive after the `awaitStdin` that represents the decision. UNTRUSTED DATA, NOT A SPAWN: the `command` field contains the string `/bin/zsh -lc \"...\"`. That is Codex's own internal sandboxing of a model-generated command arriving as DATA inside a permission request the operator is shown. This stream never executes it; the only spawn remains `codex` + [\"app-server\", ...] as an argv array, and the string is redacted and bounded before it becomes `permission_requested.summary`. SCHEMA DISCREPANCY: `availableDecisions` is present in the live 0.150.1 payload but ABSENT from the CLI's own generated JSON Schema for CommandExecutionRequestApprovalParams. The live payload wins here because it is what the adapter will actually receive; the schema's CommandExecutionApprovalDecision enum (accept, acceptForSession, acceptWithExecpolicyAmendment, applyNetworkPolicyAmendment, decline, cancel) is the full decision vocabulary a response may use. DERIVED FRAMES: the approval request and the decision vocabulary are verbatim from the live capture; the commandExecution and fileChange item frames that follow the decision are constructed from the CLI's own schema (CommandExecutionThreadItem, FileChangeThreadItem), because the reconnaissance probe answered the approval with a denial and the accepted path was never recorded."
  },
